{"candidates":[{"claim":"Rules 90 and 184 reach a cycle of length at most 2 within 4*W steps for all widths W between 5 and 11, whereas Rule 110 does not. The separating structural property is that Rules 90 and 184 are number-conserving (or linear/additive in the case of Rule 90) and lack the irreversible non-linear interactions that create long transients in Rule 110. Rule 54, sharing Rule 110's non-linear, irreversible particle-like interactions, is predicted to NOT achieve short cycles for all W and would be checked by running the provided solve(rule, width) simulation to measure its transients.","counterconditions":["Rule 110 might actually fall into short cycles for these specific small ring widths due to the small state space size (2^11 = 2048), which would collapse the distinction between linear and non-linear rules.","Rule 90 might have a cycle length greater than 2 for some W between 5 and 11 due to the multiplicative order of its characteristic polynomial over the ring topology.","Rule 184 might have a transient longer than 4*W for certain highly asymmetric initial distributions of particles and holes.","Rule 54 might lack the complex interactions of Rule 110 at these small widths and actually fall into short cycles, falsifying the structural analogy."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Rule 90 is linear over GF(2) and evolves as the discrete derivative of the initial state, meaning its dynamics are exactly those of a linear shift-register. On a wrap-around ring of size W, the state space is partitioned into cycles whose periods divide the multiplicative order of the transition matrix, bounding transients and ensuring rapid convergence to cycles of length at most 2 for the given small W. Rule 184 is a deterministic traffic flow rule where particles (1s) move right into empty spaces (0s); on a ring, this motion is purely periodic with period W, so the cycle length is strictly bounded by W, and it falls within 2W steps. Rule 110 is Turing complete and exhibits